          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "Anxiety is assessed through questionnaires that help identify the severity of symptoms and prompt further diagnostic assessments. These assessments include ruling out physical causes, reviewing any substance use, and checking for co-occurring conditions like depression, as both anxiety and depression are commonly linked. This process is described in the document titled \"Generalized anxiety disorder\" from the general clinical reference.\n\nFor anxiety management, first-line treatments include cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and pharmacotherapy with certain types of antidepressants (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors or serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors). This guidance comes from the document \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care\".\n\nRegarding your question about insomnia, it is often assessed through a sleep diary that captures the sleep pattern over time. Insomnia can be associated with anxiety, and cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia is the first-line recommendation. This approach seems to be more effective long-term compared to medications. The information on insomnia treatment is sourced from the document \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\".\n\nI couldn't cover specific medications or doses, and I did not provide detailed management strategies for co-occurring conditions. If you have ongoing symptoms, it would be best to discuss them with a healthcare professional for personalized advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
